### What does this question ask?

The interviewer wants set intersection and union using streams. This tests filter with set membership, Stream.concat, distinct, and the performance of the lookup collection.

### How do you find the intersection?

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.*;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class IntersectionUnion {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<String> branch1 = List.of(
            "C001", "C002", "C003", "C004", "C005");
        List<String> branch2 = List.of(
            "C003", "C004", "C005", "C006", "C007");

        Set<String> setB = new HashSet<>(branch2);

        List<String> intersection = branch1.stream()
            .filter(setB::contains)
            .distinct()
            .toList();

        System.out.println("Intersection: " + intersection);
    }
}
```

```text
Intersection: [C003, C004, C005]
```

### How do you find the union?

```java
import java.util.stream.Stream;

List<String> union = Stream.concat(
        branch1.stream(), branch2.stream())
    .distinct()
    .toList();

System.out.println("Union: " + union);
```

```text
Union: [C001, C002, C003, C004, C005, C006, C007]
```

### Why use a HashSet for the membership test?

| Lookup collection | contains time | Intersection time |
|---|---|---|
| List (ArrayList) | O(n) per call | O(m * n) total |
| HashSet | O(1) per call | O(m + n) total |

```java
// WRONG: quadratic with list.contains
List<String> slow = branch1.stream()
    .filter(branch2::contains)  // O(n) per element!
    .toList();

// CORRECT: linear with HashSet.contains
Set<String> fast = new HashSet<>(branch2);
List<String> quick = branch1.stream()
    .filter(fast::contains)  // O(1) per element
    .toList();
```

### How do you find the difference (A minus B)?

```java
Set<String> setB = new HashSet<>(branch2);

List<String> difference = branch1.stream()
    .filter(id -> !setB.contains(id))
    .toList();

System.out.println("Only in branch1: " + difference);
```

```text
Only in branch1: [C001, C002]
```

### How do you find the symmetric difference?

```java
Set<String> setA = new HashSet<>(branch1);
Set<String> setBB = new HashSet<>(branch2);

List<String> symmetricDiff = Stream.concat(
        branch1.stream().filter(id -> !setBB.contains(id)),
        branch2.stream().filter(id -> !setA.contains(id)))
    .toList();

System.out.println("Symmetric difference: " + symmetricDiff);
```

```text
Symmetric difference: [C001, C002, C006, C007]
```

### What are common mistakes?

Mistake: using list.contains instead of set.contains.

```java
// WRONG: O(m * n) quadratic on large lists
branch1.stream().filter(branch2::contains).toList();

// CORRECT: O(m + n) with HashSet
Set<String> setB = new HashSet<>(branch2);
branch1.stream().filter(setB::contains).toList();
```

Mistake: forgetting that equals/hashCode must be correct.

```java
// If elements are custom objects without equals/hashCode,
// HashSet.contains uses Object identity, not value equality.
// Always override equals and hashCode for value types.
```

### What about preserving order?

The intersection preserves the order of the first list. The union preserves encounter order because distinct keeps the first occurrence.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- "How do you count the intersection size?" Replace toList with count().
- "How do you find common elements across three lists?" Chain two intersections: intersect(a, b) then intersect(result, c).
- "How do you find the Jaccard similarity?" intersection.size() / union.size().
